title: Running a reconcile
tags: [[Reconcile]]
kind: guide
summary: mf reconcile or reconcile:run — a task you can follow and stop; what it prints, and how to confirm a candidate.

```
mf -n music reconcile                  # waits, drawing its progress
mf -n music reconcile --no-wait        # prints the task id and returns
mf -n music reconcile --threshold 0.7  # also look for renamed-and-edited files
```

It prints a summary and the candidate list:

```
created: 2  moved: 1

Candidates (confirm with: mf set <uuid> 'mfr_path:tree_ref=<parent_uuid>/<name>' --force):
  abc…  /music/jazz/old.mp3
      → /music2/jazz_copy.mp3   (partial_hash)
      → /backup/unknown.mp3     (size)
```

* ''created'' — metarecords made for files and directories that had none;
* ''moved'' — orphans found again with certainty and followed;
* ''candidates'' — orphans with possible matches that reconcile does not decide ([[Moves and candidates]]). Nothing is confirmed for you: set the orphan's `mfr_path` to the chosen location (a forced write, `mfr_path` being reserved). Until then the same candidate comes back at every reconcile, and the new file is not created — it waits.

Options: `--metarecord <uuid>` reconciles one subtree ([[Scoped reconcile]]);
`--threshold <0..1>` turns on the similarity phase ([[File similarity]]); `--no-mime`,
`--no-metadata`, `--no-refresh` skip those phases; `--json` prints the raw result;
`--poll-interval` sets how often progress is read (default from the CLI configuration).

In the GUI, `reconcile:run` reconciles the active repository; its progress is in the
task bar and the result lands in the status bar ([[Status bar and task bar]]) (`Reconcile: 3 created, 1 moved, 2
candidates.`) and the message panel. From the metarecord detail panel,
`metarecord:reconcile` reconciles the selected directory's subtree, and
`metarecord:reconcile watch` sets `mf_watch = true` on it first — the one-gesture way
to start tracking a folder.

A reconcile is an observable, stoppable task ([[Task kinds]], [[Cancelling a task]]);
only one runs at a time on a repository (a second is refused with `409`). Its phases:
`index`, `scan`, `fingerprint`, `similarity`, `create`, `refresh`, `mime`,
`metadata` ([[Task status and progress]]).
